experiment: axisamp
  the question      §41 run 2e. Every Timbre instrument renders at
                    amplitude 1.0. The creature's ear receives
                    0.607 * 0.50 = 0.303 when it is PRODUCING (0.223 over all
                    frames, half of which are silent). Does the 2AFC bar
                    verdict on the bandwidths survive being measured where
                    the creature actually lives?
  why it is at risk axisear v2 called bw1/bw2 SUB-BAR at d' 0.781/0.875
                    against 1.0 -- a 12% shortfall, measured 4.5x too loud,
                    on a ruler axislev showed gets BETTER as amplitude
                    falls (+8.9% in d' per halving). Two halvings is ~18%
                    and 0.875 x 1.18 = 1.03, which CLEARS it.
  what is NOT at    any d' compared BETWEEN arms at one amplitude: both
  risk              arms moved together. Only ABSOLUTE-threshold claims are
                    exposed, and "d' >= 1.0" is exactly one.
  the derivation    babble prints both means; self_gain is a genome field;
                    audio.cpp passes their PRODUCT to the larynx. The
                    PRODUCING mean is the right conditioning for an absolute
                    audibility claim -- silent frames carry no utterance --
                    and it is measured, not estimated from bin centres. It
                    matters: the all-frames point crosses one more axis.

  d' BY AMPLITUDE, each axis in its own gain cell
    amp           f0      f1      f2      f3     bw1     bw2     bw3
    1.000 <-arch    7.238   6.881   9.469   4.852   0.781   0.875   0.588
    0.500           5.418   7.493   9.964   5.331   0.840   1.001   0.694
    0.305           4.844   7.976   9.445   4.487   0.930   1.101   0.755
