  CLAUSE 1 -- IS IT MONOTONE? axislev measured only 1.0 -> 0.5 and I
  extrapolated a per-halving RATE from it. If d' turns over inside this
  ladder that extrapolation was invalid, whatever the verdict below.
    f1     47.345  56.144  63.609  70.612  83.337   -> monotone rising
    f2     89.670  99.281  89.212  73.343  37.475   -> TURNS OVER

  CLAUSE 2 -- THE TEST: the 2AFC bar (d' >= 1.0) at the archive's 1.0 and
  at the creature's 0.303. An axis that crosses means the published verdict
  was an artefact of the instrument's amplitude, not a fact about the ear.
    f1    amp 1.0: d'  6.881 AUDIBLE     amp 0.305: d'  7.976 AUDIBLE    -> same verdict
    f2    amp 1.0: d'  9.469 AUDIBLE     amp 0.305: d'  9.445 AUDIBLE    -> same verdict
    f3    amp 1.0: d'  4.852 AUDIBLE     amp 0.305: d'  4.487 AUDIBLE    -> same verdict
    bw1   amp 1.0: d'  0.781 sub-bar     amp 0.305: d'  0.930 sub-bar    -> same verdict
    bw2   amp 1.0: d'  0.875 sub-bar     amp 0.305: d'  1.101 AUDIBLE    -> *** CROSSES
    bw3   amp 1.0: d'  0.588 sub-bar     amp 0.305: d'  0.755 sub-bar    -> same verdict

  NOTE: the trend is not monotone across this ladder, so axislev's
  per-halving rate does not extrapolate and the 18% figure in the header
  of this experiment is withdrawn. The verdict below stands on the
  MEASURED rung rather than on that extrapolation, which is why the test
  was run at the creature's amplitude instead of computed from a slope.

  1 AXIS CROSSES THE BAR AT THE AMPLITUDE THE CREATURE ACTUALLY HEARS, so
  axisear v2's "REAL BUT SUB-BAR" was an artefact of rendering 4.5x too
  loud. The published verdict is CORRECTED: at the creature's own level
  those axes are audible by the same 2AFC bar that called them sub-bar.
  THE AXIS COUNT CHANGES, and so does the ship decision -- re-derive the
  prize before quoting it, and note every ABSOLUTE d' threshold in this
  archive was set at 1.0 and needs the same re-reading.
